 I  think that show is in the quality of the  code the quality and number of  contributors where they're coming from  we have contributors from every time  zone on the planet from almost every  continent I still haven't proved whether  we have someone in Antarctica or not  during Antarctica and you're  contributing to communities call me this  has been a really important part the  vendors are critical piece of our  ecosystem kubernetes would not be  successful without the ecosystem we knew  this from the beginning and I would  happy to say that we're really  successful there but it is still a  community driven project that is not  tied or locked into any one vendor while  we have a ton of people at Google who  are working on kubernetes myself  included they are not exclusively  focusing on Google's work they're  working to build the project a bit large  and you have you've got a lot of insight  with with a lot of customers a lot of  users and a lot of community members  working with kubernetes what are some of  the trends you see emerging maybe some  unexpected trends that you are patterns  you've seen emerge so I do I talk to a  lot of customers and I've definitely  seen some emerging trends we've passed  the point where people are struggling to  figure out if they need kubernetes it's  more or less assumed now if you're  building big systems kubernetes is the  way to do it but the patterns that were  merging now are scale large size how big  can my clusters get how many pods can I  run on a node what what's the density  that I can achieve  how do I Drive my utilization we're  talking about multi cluster situations  I've got clusters around the world  around the country how do I link them  together and make them work together  some of these problems are sort of at  the very edges of kubernetes itself but  I think there's a lot we can do within  kubernetes to make it easier to build  solutions that are solving these  problems hybrid is a really hot topic  right now 